---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../site';
import { utilities } from '../data/utilities';
---
<Base
  title="MacPeek Privacy: No Account, No Telemetry, Data Stays on Your Mac"
  description="MacPeek is private by design: no account, no telemetry, no network access. See exactly what each utility reads from your Mac and what access it needs."
  jsonLd={{ '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'WebPage', name: 'MacPeek privacy', url: new URL('/privacy/', Astro.site).toString() }}
>
  <div class="wrap narrow article">
    <div class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> › Privacy</div>
    <h1>MacPeek is private by design</h1>
    <p class="lede">MacPeek does not send process, port, display, network or any other system information to a server.</p>
    <ul class="checks">
      <li><strong>No account.</strong> Nothing to sign in to.</li>
      <li><strong>No telemetry or analytics</strong> in the app.</li>
      <li><strong>No cloud service</strong> required or used: no update pings, no crash reporting.</li>
      <li><strong>No network connection</strong> is used by any shipped feature.</li>
      <li><strong>Local operation.</strong> Data is held in memory to draw the screen and is not written to disk. The only thing stored is your preferences (which utilities are on, refresh interval, toggles) in macOS user defaults.</li>
      <li><strong>Open source.</strong> <a href={SITE.github}>Verify it yourself.</a></li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What each utility reads</h2>
    <p>Utilities marked <em>Coming soon</em> are not in the current release; this is what they are planned to read, and the list will be re-checked against the code before each ships.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Utility</th><th>Reads (locally)</th><th>Access needed</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {utilities.map((u) => (
          <tr>
            <td><a href={`/utilities/${u.id}/`}>{u.name}</a>{u.status === 'available' ? '' : ' (coming soon)'}</td>
            <td>{u.reads.join('; ')}</td>
            <td>{u.access}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Two planned utilities, NetPeek and DNSPeek, measure latency and DNS lookups. When they ship they will send test requests <em>only when you ask them to run a check</em>, and never any information about your Mac. Nothing in the current release makes network requests.</p>
    <p>MacPeek never asks for administrator rights and never bypasses macOS security controls.</p>

    <h2>This website</h2>
    <p>The site is static. It sets no cookies and loads no third-party scripts. The only thing it stores in your browser is your light/dark theme choice, locally. If privacy-friendly analytics are ever added, they will be documented here first.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
